---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const arrheniusCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'exp(-6/t)', label: 'reaction with Ea = 6 (kT units)', color: '#d9480f', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: 'exp(-3/t)', label: 'easier reaction (Ea = 3)', color: '#1f6f54' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 't', label: 'temperature (kT units)', min: 0.2, max: 2, step: 0.1, value: 0.5 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 6, label: '— (readouts drive the curve)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 1, label: 'relative rate constant' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 330,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/intermediate/reaction-kinetics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Thermodynamics says whether a reaction <em>can</em> go; kinetics says <strong>how fast it
    actually goes</strong> — and they disagree constantly. Petrol + oxygen is thermodynamically
    thrilled and kinetically bored at room temperature (no spark, no fire). Kinetics is the study
    of the in-between: collision energy, molecular orientation, and the activation hill.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Arrhenius in action — heat the system and the rate constant climbs exponentially, steeply for hard reactions" widget="plotter" config={arrheniusCfg} />

  <p>
    Arrhenius's law (1889): k = A·e^(−Ea/kT). The readout slider is temperature — drag it and
    watch rates multiply, not add. The kitchen rule of thumb holds: roughly 10 °C warmer
    <strong>doubles</strong> most reaction rates, which is why fridges preserve food and fever
    burns through your metabolic reserves.
  </p>

  <h2>Rate laws and their machinery</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rate laws come from experiment, not the equation.</strong> H₂ + Br₂ → HBr looks simple but has a fiendish rate law — mechanism and stoichiometry are different stories.</li>
    <li><strong>Reaction order:</strong> if doubling a concentration doubles the rate, order 1; quadruples, order 2. Half-life behaviour follows: first-order reactions (radioactive decay, many drug clearances) have a constant half-life.</li>
    <li><strong>Catalysts lower the hill.</strong> Enzymes are catalysts that can accelerate reactions 10⁶–10²⁰ fold by holding molecules in exactly the right orientation — no catalyst, no life at body temperature.</li>
    <li><strong>Rate-determining step:</strong> a multi-step reaction runs at the speed of its slowest step — the molecular traffic jam that mechanisms reveal.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Arrhenius (1889); Atkins, Physical Chemistry (Kinetics); enzyme rates: Michaelis & Menten (1913).</p>
</TopicPage>
